/* std/sync: the mutex surface (B-5). `Mutex<T>` itself is a compiler builtin,
   in the shape `Atomic<T>`/`Thread<T>` have: a monomorphized per-element-type
   representation, not `Copy`, with its method set lowered to the C runtime's
   pthread mutex rather than written in Kelp. Its surface is

       fun Mutex.new(value: move T).R: Mutex<T>
       fun Mutex<T>.lock(block: scoped fun(mut T).R: R).R: R
       fun Mutex<T>.into_inner().R: T

   `Mutex.new` takes the guarded value by move. `lock` runs a scoped block while
   the mutex is held: the block receives `v: mut T`, a mutable borrow that is
   valid only inside the block (the existing scoped-block rules refuse storing
   or returning it), the block's value is returned, and the mutex is released on
   every exit of the block. A panic inside the block releases it too and marks
   the mutex POISONED, so a later `lock` panics with "mutex poisoned"; there is
   no unwinding, so the panicking thread's frame is abandoned
   (`plan/mk9-memory-model.md` section 2). `into_inner` consumes the mutex and
   returns the value it held.

   `Mutex<T>` is `Sync`: a shared borrow of it may cross a thread boundary when
   `T` is `Send`-shaped, exactly as a `&Atomic<T>` may. It is hosted only: a
   freestanding manifest refuses it, because the runtime half is a pthread mutex
   (a future spin-lock option is what a kernel would use instead).

   The two calls have no `.kp` declarations for the reason `std/atomic`
   documents the atomic methods it declares nowhere: the compiler builds their
   signatures at the call site and the C runtime lowers them. This module exists
   so a program can name the surface it is using (`impl "std/sync"`), and so the
   module list in `plan/` has the one id the mutex belongs to.

   The rest of the family lives here too, all builtin in the same shape:

       fun RwLock.new(value: move T).R: RwLock<T>
       fun RwLock<T>.read(block: scoped fun(&T).R: R).R: R
       fun RwLock<T>.write(block: scoped fun(mut T).R: R).R: R
       fun RwLock<T>.try_read(block: scoped fun(&T).R: R).R: Option<R>
       fun RwLock<T>.try_write(block: scoped fun(mut T).R: R).R: Option<R>
       fun RwLock<T>.into_inner().R: T

   `RwLock<T>` is writer-preferring: many readers at once, one writer alone,
   and a waiting writer blocks new readers so a stream of readers cannot starve
   it. A panic inside `write` poisons it; a panic inside `read` releases the
   read without poisoning, because a reader never writes the value. The `try_*`
   forms run their block only when the lock was free and hand the value back in
   `Option` (`None` when it was busy).

       fun Condvar.new().R: Condvar
       fun Condvar.notify_one()
       fun Condvar.notify_all()
       fun Mutex<T>.wait_while(cv: &Condvar, pred: fun(&T).R: bool, block: scoped fun(mut T).R: R).R: R
       fun Mutex<T>.wait_while_timeout(cv: &Condvar, pred: fun(&T).R: bool, millis: i64, block: scoped fun(mut T).R: R).R: Option<R>

   A wait has to release the mutex's own lock atomically around the sleep,
   which a scoped guard cannot express, so the wait lives on the mutex:
   `m.wait_while(cv, pred) { v -> ... }` re-tests `pred(&v)` after every wakeup
   (spurious wakeups are the library's problem) and then runs the block with
   the lock held. There is no bare `wait`. The timed form is `None` when the
   deadline passed with the predicate still true, `Some` otherwise.

   `m.share()` and `cv.share()` return another owned handle to the same
   runtime record (a reference count; the last handle's drop frees it), and
   `m.swap(value)` exchanges the guarded value in one locked step. Channels,
   below, and `std/pool` are built from them.

   Every record here is a pthread object, so a freestanding manifest refuses
   each by name (`plan/sync.md`). */

/* Channels, in pure Kelp over the builtins above (`plan/sync.md` section 3,
   `plan/st1-status.md` for the shape that was chosen):

       fun sync.bounded<T>(cap: usize).R: (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>)
       fun sync.unbounded<T>().R: (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>)
       fun Sender<T>.send(value: move T).R: option.Option<T>
       fun Sender<T>.clone().R: Sender<T>
       fun Receiver<T>.recv().R: result.Result<T, RecvErr>
       fun Receiver<T>.try_recv().R: option.Option<T>
       fun Receiver<T>.recv_timeout(millis: i64).R: result.Result<T, RecvTimeout>
       for v in rx { ... }

   There is no new runtime record. The two ends share one queue and one state
   record through `Mutex.share()` and `Condvar.share()` (a handle to the same
   runtime record, freed when the last handle drops). The queue is a
   `Mutex<List<T>>` that is only ever locked while the `ChanMeta` mutex is
   held, so the meta mutex is the real lock and the queue mutex is the typed
   cell the generic element needs; every wait is a `wait_while` on the meta
   mutex over plain counters, which keeps the predicates non-generic.

   `send` gives the value back as `Some(value)` when the receiver is gone and
   answers `None` when it was queued (so the result is must-use, and `Option`
   is the one payload shape that needs no `unit` value). A bounded send waits
   while the queue is full. `recv` waits while the queue is empty and a sender
   is alive; once the queue is empty and every sender is gone it answers
   `Err(RecvErr.Closed)`. Closing is done by two non-generic `Drop` types
   (`SendEnd`, `RecvEnd`) each end owns: a type parameter cannot carry a
   destructor, so the destructor lives on the part that has none. Their `live`
   field is `false` for the zeroed value a partial move leaves behind, which
   is what lets `val (tx, rx) = sync.bounded(4)` take the pair apart.

   `for v in rx` works because `Receiver<T>` declares `next` on its template;
   the compiler walks any generic `datac` of that shape like an `Iterator`
   (a trait impl cannot name a generic instantiation yet). Everything here is
   hosted only: the module is not in the freestanding admission table, and its
   `Mutex` and `Condvar` are refused there by name. */
impl "std/option"
impl "std/result"

// Why `recv` has no value: every sender is gone and the queue is empty.
enum RecvErr {
    Closed
}

// Why `recv_timeout` has no value.
enum RecvTimeout {
    Timeout
    Closed
}

// Everything the two ends agree on, guarded by one mutex. `count` mirrors the
// queue's length, so the waits never need to look inside the generic queue.
datac ChanMeta(var count: usize, var senders: i64, var rx_alive: bool, val cap: usize, val bounded: bool)

// What a sender owns besides the queue, and the destructor that closes the
// channel when the last one goes.
datac SendEnd(val live: bool, val meta: Mutex<ChanMeta>, val ne: Condvar, val nf: Condvar)

datac RecvEnd(val live: bool, val meta: Mutex<ChanMeta>, val ne: Condvar, val nf: Condvar)

datac Sender<T>(val q: Mutex<List<T>>, val end: SendEnd)

datac Receiver<T>(val q: Mutex<List<T>>, val end: RecvEnd)

fun mut SendEnd.drop() {
    if !live {
        return
    }
    val last = meta.lock { s ->
        s.senders -= 1
        s.senders == 0
    }
    if last {
        ne.notify_all()
    }
}

fun mut RecvEnd.drop() {
    if !live {
        return
    }
    meta.lock { s -> s.rx_alive = false }
    nf.notify_all()
}

fun make_channel<T>(cap: usize, bounded: bool).R: (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>) {
    val meta = Mutex.new(ChanMeta(count = 0, senders = 1, rx_alive = true, cap = cap, bounded = bounded))
    val q: Mutex<List<T>> = Mutex.new(List.empty())
    val ne = Condvar.new()
    val nf = Condvar.new()
    val rx = Receiver<T>(q = q.share(), end = RecvEnd(live = true, meta = meta.share(), ne = ne.share(), nf = nf.share()))
    val tx = Sender<T>(q = q, end = SendEnd(live = true, meta = meta, ne = ne, nf = nf))
    return (tx, rx)
}

// A channel that holds at most `cap` values: `send` waits while it is full.
fun bounded<T>(cap: usize).R: (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>) {
    if cap == 0 {
        panic("sync.bounded: the capacity must be at least 1")
    }
    return make_channel<T>(cap, true)
}

// A channel with no limit: `send` never waits.
fun unbounded<T>().R: (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>) {
    return make_channel<T>(0, false)
}

// Queue `value`. `None` means it was queued; `Some(value)` hands it back because
// the receiver is gone. A scoped block cannot capture an owned value by move, so
// the value waits in a private one-element mutex and the block swaps it across.
fun Sender<T>.send(value: move T).R: option.Option<T> {
    val staged: Mutex<List<T>> = Mutex.new(List.of(value))
    val r = self.end.meta.wait_while(&self.end.nf, fun(s: &ChanMeta).R: bool = s.bounded && s.count >= s.cap && s.rx_alive) { s ->
        var mine = staged.swap(List.empty())
        if s.rx_alive {
            s.count += 1
            var queue = self.q.swap(List.empty())
            queue.push(mine.remove(0))
            val none = self.q.swap(queue)
            val sent: option.Option<T> = option.Option.None
            sent
        } else {
            option.Option.Some(value = mine.remove(0))
        }
    }
    self.end.ne.notify_one()
    return r
}

// Another sender on the same channel; the channel closes when the last one drops.
fun Sender<T>.clone().R: Sender<T> {
    self.end.meta.lock { s -> s.senders += 1 }
    return Sender<T>(q = self.q.share(), end = SendEnd(live = true, meta = self.end.meta.share(), ne = self.end.ne.share(), nf = self.end.nf.share()))
}

// Take the oldest value, waiting while the queue is empty and a sender is alive.
fun Receiver<T>.recv().R: result.Result<T, RecvErr> {
    val r = self.end.meta.wait_while(&self.end.ne, fun(s: &ChanMeta).R: bool = s.count == 0 && s.senders > 0) { s ->
        if s.count > 0 {
            s.count -= 1
            val item = self.q.lock { l -> l.remove(0) }
            val got: result.Result<T, RecvErr> = result.Result.Ok(value = item)
            got
        } else {
            val closed: result.Result<T, RecvErr> = result.Result.Err(error = RecvErr.Closed)
            closed
        }
    }
    self.end.nf.notify_one()
    return r
}

// The oldest value if there is one now; never waits.
fun Receiver<T>.try_recv().R: option.Option<T> {
    val r = self.end.meta.lock { s ->
        if s.count > 0 {
            s.count -= 1
            val item = self.q.lock { l -> l.remove(0) }
            option.Option.Some(value = item)
        } else {
            val none: option.Option<T> = option.Option.None
            none
        }
    }
    self.end.nf.notify_one()
    return r
}

// Like `recv`, but gives up after `millis` milliseconds with `Err(Timeout)`.
fun Receiver<T>.recv_timeout(millis: i64).R: result.Result<T, RecvTimeout> {
    val r = self.end.meta.wait_while_timeout(&self.end.ne, fun(s: &ChanMeta).R: bool = s.count == 0 && s.senders > 0, millis) { s ->
        if s.count > 0 {
            s.count -= 1
            val item = self.q.lock { l -> l.remove(0) }
            val got: result.Result<T, RecvTimeout> = result.Result.Ok(value = item)
            got
        } else {
            val closed: result.Result<T, RecvTimeout> = result.Result.Err(error = RecvTimeout.Closed)
            closed
        }
    }
    self.end.nf.notify_one()
    return when r {
        Some(v) -> v
        None -> {
            val late: result.Result<T, RecvTimeout> = result.Result.Err(error = RecvTimeout.Timeout)
            late
        }
    }
}

// `for v in rx`: one value per pass until every sender is gone.
fun mut Receiver<T>.next().R: option.Option<T> {
    return when self.recv() {
        Ok(v) -> option.Option.Some(value = v)
        Err(_) -> {
            val done: option.Option<T> = option.Option.None
            done
        }
    }
}
